## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical theming, context usage, cross‑platform styling, and persistence basics.

## Key Concepts
- ThemeContext and useContext
- useColorScheme/Appearance API
- AsyncStorage for persistence
- Themed components pattern
- Basic testing strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
import React, {createContext, useContext, useState, useEffect} from 'react'

const defaultTheme = {
  background: '#ffffff', text: '#000000', primary: '#1f6feb'
}

const ThemeContext = createContext(defaultTheme)

export const ThemeProvider = ({children}) => {
  const colorScheme = useColorScheme()
  const [theme, setTheme] = useState(defaultTheme)

  useEffect(() => {
    const load = async () => {
      const saved = await AsyncStorage.getItem('app_theme')
      if (saved) setTheme(JSON.parse(saved))
      else if (colorScheme === 'dark') setTheme({background: '#000000', text: '#ffffff', primary: '#4dabf7'})
    }
    load()
  }, [])

  const toggle = async () => {
    const next = theme.background === '#ffffff'
      ? {background: '#000000', text: '#ffffff', primary: '#4dabf7'}
      : {background: '#ffffff', text: '#000000', primary: '#1f6feb'}
    setTheme(next)
    await AsyncStorage.setItem('app_theme', JSON.stringify(next))
  }

  return (
    <ThemeContext.Provider value={{theme, toggle}}>
      {children}
    </ThemeContext.Provider>
  )
}

export const ThemedButton = ({title, onPress}) => {
  const {theme} = useContext(ThemeContext)
  return (
    <TouchableOpacity onPress={onPress} style={{backgroundColor: theme.primary, padding: 12, borderRadius: 6}}>
      <Text style={{color: theme.text}}>{title}</Text>
    </TouchableOpacity>
  )
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross‑platform theming (unit and snapshot) and simulate system color changes? 
- How would you extend to per‑component theming or accessibility (increase contrast, font scale)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests expertise in offline-first synchronization, CRDT conflict resolution, and native module design that preserves UI performance. Probes threading, memory, and power trade-offs in a real-world React Native data-sync scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first CRDT data synchronization
- TurboModule/JSI bridge design
- Background thread execution vs UI thread
- Backpressure, tombstone GC, and memory budgeting

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal API sketch
const engine = await init({ peerId: 'device-1', maxLog: 1000 });

engine.onRemoteChange((change) => {
  // Merge remote changes into local state
  console.log('Received:', change);
});

// Apply local drawing operations
engine.applyLocalChange({ type: 'stroke', points: [...], color: '#000' });

// Ensure changes are persisted and synced
await engine.flush();

// Clean up when done
engine.stopSync();
```

## Conflict Resolution
- Last-writer-wins for scalar values; CRDT counters/g-sets for concurrent edits
- Auto-merge without user intervention; conflicts exposed only when semantically impossible to resolve

## Memory Footprint
- Tombstone GC prunes deleted entries above threshold
- Inbox cap (e.g., 1000 pending ops) with spilling to disk
- Lazy loading of historical states

## Battery Impact
- Background thread with coarse-grained intervals (every 5-15s)
- Exponential backoff on failures; WiFi-only mode option
- Backpressure: pause sync when screen off or low battery
